title: 'Decided by Jeff: the root too. From an author''s perspective, a root that author overrides shows the override.'
pid: 517002aa-ec78-452d-9378-4fa0faaceedb
body: |2-
  His answer to Top's question, under "the root itself is never
  replaced": a note titled "the root too". So each root stands for its
  original, the node its '(overridesHere N)' names, else itself; from
  an author's perspective it shows that author's overrider of the
  original, marked '(overridesHere N)'; from the root authors', the
  original, raw ('substitute_roots' in 'server/refresh/visit.rs'). The
  consequences, each decided:
  - The view stays what it was: its view ID, which is all that names a
    view to the server, and so what saves and reconnects use; the
    buffer keeps its name. The mode line names the perspective.
  - The root is re-pointed where it stands, so it keeps its viewnode ID,
    and point on it stays, both ways; its children follow from its own
    visit. Cycling back to the root authors' shows the original again.
  - The root authors' perspective counts a root's author as its
    original's, not the override's, so a replaced root never changes
    whose the default is.
  - A fork of the root no longer marks the clone '(overridesHere F)'.
    The docs already said the fork re-roots the view at the clone; with
    the mark, the root authors' would read the clone as a stand-in for F
    and show F raw again. Jeff saw the mark as 'ĥ' on such a root.
  - A search buffer's hits are its roots, so a switched search shows
    each author's override of a hit.
